<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Italian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Italian colonial empire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Italian Empire was the colonial empire of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>, built from the late nineteenth century until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. It included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and, from 1936, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. It has its own profile on the site and collapsed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s defeat in the Second World War.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italian-empire.jpg" alt="Italian Empire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F l a n k e r · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Italy_(1861-1946)_crowned.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, unified as a nation-state in the late 19th century, wished to compete with the other European powers and saw its interests in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>. It arrived late and depended on the acquiescence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. When France imposed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> in May 1881, what the Italian press called the Slap of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> left Italy isolated and led it to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-alliance-1882">Triple Alliance</a> in 1882 with Germany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>.</p><p>The genesis of the empire was the purchase in 1869 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assab-bay">Assab Bay</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> by an Italian navigation company wanting a coaling station as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> opened. The government took it over in 1882, making it modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s first overseas territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in 1915 after being promised territorial spoils, mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Albania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> saw Italian occupations during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. In 1923 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a> formalised Italian administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese</a>. Mussolini, in power from 1922, sought to enlarge the empire, and in 1939 drew up a document, The March to the Oceans, arguing that countries without free access to the high seas were not independent. By 1939 Italian settlers numbered 120,000 to 150,000 in Libya and 165,000 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-east-africa">Italian East Africa</a>, where the government encouraged systematic demographic colonisation.</p><p>At its peak, between 1936 and 1941, the colonial possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> covered present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, the last three grouped as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-east-africa">Africa Orientale Italiana</a>. Outside Africa Italy held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese Islands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> and had a concession at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1940 and occupied British Somaliland, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Italian Army was expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> by British and Commonwealth forces in 1941, and by the autumn of 1943 the empire had effectively ended. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> was deposed on 25 July 1943, and an armistice with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> was made public on 8 September, after which German forces ended Italian rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese</a>. In 1947 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> relinquished its claims on its former colonies. In 1950 the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Italian Somaliland</a> became a UN trust territory under Italian administration, which lasted until its independence in 1960.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Italian Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Italy Benito Mussolini’s 20-year dictatorship brought a highly aggressive nationalism, wars, an <b>Italian Empire</b> and alliance with Hitler.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
